WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.740
A dog eats a meal.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.400
The food falls out of an opening in its neck before it reaches the stomach.

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.000
Yet the stomach pours out acid juice.

4
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.200
What told the stomach to start?

5
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.260
The answer came from Pavlov's laboratory in Saint Petersburg.

6
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:24.273
Pavlov's laboratory opened the dog's esophagus at the neck, so swallowed food fell

7
00:00:24.273 --> 00:00:28.093
out. A gastric fistula let juice drain from the stomach.

8
00:00:28.913 --> 00:00:32.713
If juice flowed only when food touched the stomach, the dog should make none.

9
00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:37.333
Instead, after a delay of some minutes, abundant acid juice flowed.

10
00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:41.777
Then both vagus nerves were cut.

11
00:00:42.597 --> 00:00:45.077
The vagus nerves connect the brainstem to the gut.

12
00:00:45.997 --> 00:00:47.497
Now eating no longer brought the juice.

13
00:00:48.377 --> 00:00:52.977
So in the dog, a nerve-driven cephalic phase exists, carried by the vagus.

14
00:00:53.957 --> 00:00:56.397
Sight, smell, taste and chewing of food trigger it.

15
00:00:57.117 --> 00:01:01.517
It does not rule out later phases, which run once food is in the stomach or

16
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:02.517
intestine.

17
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.210
Later phases are partly chemical.

18
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:11.010
A hormone is a substance released into the blood that acts on distant target cells.

19
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:17.050
Gastrin is one. G cells in the stomach lining release gastrin when food, especially

20
00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.050
protein, is in the stomach.

21
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.930
Gastrin makes the stomach secrete more acid.

22
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:27.423
Acid chyme then enters the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine.

23
00:01:28.343 --> 00:01:29.923
Its lining releases secretin.

24
00:01:30.783 --> 00:01:35.483
Secretin makes pancreatic duct cells secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid, which

25
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:37.143
neutralizes the stomach's acid.

26
00:01:38.123 --> 00:01:40.963
Fats and proteins in the duodenum bring out CCK.

27
00:01:41.843 --> 00:01:47.163
CCK drives the pancreas to release enzymes, and the gallbladder to contract and

28
00:01:47.163 --> 00:01:48.163
release bile.

29
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:50.553
Nerves and hormones overlap.

30
00:01:51.773 --> 00:01:55.033
Healthy human volunteers were given CCK-8 at one dose.

31
00:01:56.033 --> 00:02:00.353
Atropine blocks the acetylcholine receptors that nerves use on many glands.

32
00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:05.113
With atropine, the pancreatic trypsin response fell by 84%.

33
00:02:05.113 --> 00:02:10.553
percent. So at that dose, much of the hormone's effect depended on acetylcholine,

34
00:02:10.973 --> 00:02:14.193
and tests on rat pancreas suggested it came from nerves.

35
00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:19.333
Block one route, and the fraction that disappears shows how much depends on that

36
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:20.833
route, under those conditions.
